{"id":"360478e0-65fd-452a-a3bf-16f0f4bc1dfc","object":"chat.completion","created":1784998722,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    lines = case.get('lines', [])\\n    trajectory = []\\n    valid = True\\n    error_line = None\\n    \\n    # Allowed types and their canonical names\\n    allowed_types = {'Thought', 'Action', 'Observation', 'Final'}\\n    \\n    # Define state transitions: for each type, which types are allowed as next\\n    transitions = {\\n        'Thought': {'Action', 'Final'},\\n        'Action': {'Observation'},\\n        'Observation': {'Thought', 'Final'},\\n        'Final': set()  # no following lines allowed\\n    }\\n    \\n    prev_type = None\\n    for i, line in enumerate(lines):\\n        # Parse line\\n        if ':' not in line:\\n            valid = False\\n            error_line = i\\n            break\\n        parts = line.split(':', 1)\\n        prefix = parts[0].strip()\\n        content = parts[1].strip() if len(parts) > 1 else ''\\n        \\n        # Validate prefix\\n        if prefix not in allowed_types:\\n            valid = False\\n            error_line = i\\n            break\\n        \\n        cur_type = prefix\\n        trajectory.append({'type': cur_type, 'content': content})\\n        \\n        # Check transition from previous\\n        if prev_type is not None:\\n            if cur_type not in transitions.get(prev_type, set()):\\n                valid = False\\n                error_line = i\\n                break\\n        else:\\n            # First line must be Thought\\n            if cur_type != 'Thought':\\n                valid = False\\n                error_line = i\\n                break\\n        \\n        prev_type = cur_type\\n    \\n    # If loop ended without break and valid still True, check that last type is Final\\n    if valid and len(lines) > 0:\\n        last_type = trajectory[-1]['type']\\n        if last_type != 'Final':\\n            valid = False\\n            error_line = len(lines) - 1\\n    \\n    # If no lines, set error_line to 0\\n    if not lines:\\n        valid = False\\n        error_line = 0\\n    \\n    return {\\n        'valid': valid,\\n        'error_line': error_line,\\n        'trajectory': trajectory\\n    }\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to understand the task based on the provided information. The task is \"AGENT-RA-01\". The output schema is given. We need to write a Python function solve(case) that processes the input payload. The payload contains \"lines\" and \"_case_nonce\". The lines appear to be a sequence of agent interaction lines: Thought, Action, Observation, Final. The task likely expects to validate a particular pattern or extract information. The output has three fields: valid (boolean), error_line (integer or null), trajectory (array of objects with content and type).\n\nLooking at the fixture cases, all three have identical lines: [\"Thought: inspect\", \"Action: lookup\", \"Observation: found\", \"Thought: answer\", \"Final: done\"]. The problem likely expects to simulate a ReAct agent reasoning and determine if the trajectory is valid according to some rules, and if not, indicate the error line.\n\nThe output schema: \n- valid: boolean\n- error_line: integer or null\n- trajectory: array of {content: string, type: string}\n\nThe trajectory likely represents the sequence of steps. The type might be \"thought\", \"action\", \"observation\", \"final\", etc. The content is the text after the colon? Possibly.\n\nGiven the lines: \nThought: inspect -> type \"thought\", content \"inspect\"\nAction: lookup -> type \"action\", content \"lookup\"\nObservation: found -> type \"observation\", content \"found\"\nThought: answer -> type \"thought\", content \"answer\"\nFinal: done -> type \"final\", content \"done\"\n\nWe need to parse each line, strip the prefix, and create trajectory entries. Then validate if the sequence is valid. A typical ReAct pattern: Thought, Action, Observation, Thought, Final. The lines are already in that order. The valid might be true if the pattern matches exactly? But there might be rules: after Action must come Observation, after Observation must come Thought or Final? The given lines exactly follow: Thought, Action, Observation, Thought, Final. That seems valid.\n\nWhat about error_line? If valid is false, we need to indicate the line index (0-based?) that caused the error. If valid is true, error_line is null.\n\nWe need to infer the expected validation. Possibly the task is to detect if the lines follow the ReAct pattern: \n- Start with Thought\n- Then Action\n- Then Observation\n- Then optionally repeat Thought/Action/Observation\n- End with Final\nAnd each line must start with one of the prefixes: \"Thought:\", \"Action:\", \"Observation:\", \"Final:\" (case-sensitive? The given lines have capital first letter, colon, space). The content after the colon.\n\nAlso maybe there are constraints on content? Not specified.\n\nThe fixture shows all three cases identical, so the solve function should return the same result for all three.\n\nWe must implement robust parsing and validation. The function must not mutate case, use only standard library, no external calls.\n\nLet's design:\n\n1. Initialize trajectory = [].\n2. Initialize valid = True, error_line = None.\n3. Iterate over lines with index i.\n4. For each line, try to split into type and content.\n   - Check that line contains a colon (maybe spaces?).\n   - Extract the prefix (before colon) and content (after colon). Trim whitespace? The examples: \"Thought: inspect\" -> prefix \"Thought\", content \"inspect\". Should we allow extra spaces? We'll trim.\n   - Recognize known types: \"Thought\", \"Action\", \"Observation\", \"Final\". If unknown type, set valid=False, error_line=i, break.\n   - Add to trajectory: {\"type\": type_lower? Actually type should be string as per output. The schema doesn't specify case; we can use the exact prefix as in the input? The examples show capital first letter. We'll use the exact prefix as given (capitalized). But to be safe, maybe use lower case? The output example not given. Considering the \"type\" in trajectory is a string, it should be consistent. The input lines have \"Thought:\", \"Action:\", etc. We can store the type as the prefix without colon, preserving case. But typical ReAct types are \"thought\", \"action\", \"observation\", \"final\". However schema doesn't enforce case. We'll store as in input: \"Thought\", \"Action\", etc.\n   - For validity, we need to enforce the expected sequence pattern.\n5. After parsing all lines, check sequence:\n   - The first line must be \"Thought\" (type). If not, error.\n   - After a \"Thought\", next can be \"Action\" or \"Final\"? In the given, after first Thought comes Action. After Action must come Observation. After Observation must come Thought or Final? Actually pattern: Thought -> Action -> Observation -> (Thought or Final) -> ... and so on. Usually after Observation you can have another Thought (to reason) or Final if done. In the sequence: Thought, Action, Observation, Thought, Final. That is valid.\n   - So we can define allowed transitions:\n     - Thought: next allowed = Action, Final (if it's the end) but after Thought, if there is more lines, should be Action? Actually after a Thought, the next could be Action (to act) or Final (if you're ready to answer). But in ReAct, you always need an Action before Observation. However sometimes you can have multiple Thoughts? Typically pattern is Thought, Action, Observation, (Thought, Action, Observation)*, Final. But you could have a Final directly after a Thought? Possibly. The given has Thought, then Final at end. So allowed transition: Thought -> Final.\n     - Action: next must be Observation.\n     - Observation: next must be Thought or Final.\n     - Final: must be last line (no lines after).\n   - Also cannot